<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  SignPath artifact configuration for the Qualia2FA release artifacts.

  It describes the structure of what is submitted for signing, not how it is signed: the
  certificate, the signing policy and the approval rules live in the SignPath project. This
  file is versioned here so that a change to the artifacts and a change to what is signed
  are reviewed together, and is uploaded to the SignPath project when it changes.

  Schema: http://signpath.io/artifact-configuration/v1

  # What arrives, and why a zip

  The release job produces two installers for one tag, and a SignPath signing request
  carries one artifact. A `<zip-file>` root is the documented way to sign several files in
  one request: the job zips `Qualia2FA-v<version>.msi` and `Qualia2FA-v<version>.exe` into
  `bundle.zip` and submits that. The `version` parameter is passed with the request, so the
  paths below are checked against the version the tag carries instead of a wildcard that
  would accept any version.

  # What is signed

  - The MSI installer itself: `<msi-file>` with a trailing `<authenticode-sign/>` signs the
    package. This is the signature Windows shows in the install dialog and the one that
    decides whether SmartScreen warns.
  - The EXE installer: jpackage builds it with Inno Setup, and the result is a Portable
    Executable, so `<pe-file>` is its element. Its payload is compressed, which is why the
    application inside cannot be signed through this file.

  # Current state

  Deep signing of the launcher inside the MSI is deliberately **not** enabled. A jpackage
  MSI lays its files out in a directory named after the package, and signing them requires
  the exact paths, which are read from an artifact sample rather than guessed: an element
  that matches nothing is accepted silently, and a configuration that quietly signs less
  than it claims is worse than one that signs less and says so. Add the paths here once a
  sample has been uploaded, and the launcher inside the installer gets a signature too.

  Signing the Rust library or the bundled runtime image is deliberately not planned: the
  runtime comes from the JDK, and putting this project's signature on a third-party binary
  would claim it as this project's own.
-->
<artifact-configuration xmlns="http://signpath.io/artifact-configuration/v1">
  <parameters>
    <!-- The application version without the tag's `v`, e.g. `0.0.2`. -->
    <parameter name="version" required="true" />
  </parameters>

  <zip-file>
    <msi-file path="Qualia2FA-v${version}.msi">
      <authenticode-sign />
    </msi-file>

    <!--
      The PE metadata below is what the signature states about the file. It is set here
      rather than in the build because jpackage does not write a product version into the
      installer's own version resource: without it the file properties of a signed installer
      would show an empty product name next to a valid signature.
    -->
    <pe-file
      path="Qualia2FA-v${version}.exe"
      product-name="Qualia2FA"
      product-version="${version}"
      file-version="${version}"
      company-name="Qualia2FA"
      copyright="Copyright (c) Qualia2FA contributors">
      <authenticode-sign />
    </pe-file>
  </zip-file>
</artifact-configuration>
